[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15350554-e886-5e11-8207-77bbb3e0bec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.93\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399421} {:answer/id #uuid "fb654c8f-375d-5fa0-b333-4fd2c3d1805a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.72\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399422} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6c956a-c96c-5420-a899-8859c8a907f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.35\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399423} {:answer/id #uuid "3a202375-39cd-502a-81cb-6cca990bb170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.06\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399424} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bc53a0-6be4-5825-a047-05acf7782f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.08\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a202375-39cd-502a-81cb-6cca990bb170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.06\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399424} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9367409-a53f-579f-8cdb-a83c5faaa2a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1af9295c-5eff-510e-ba44-71a6e28cc19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104195" :question/problem "A gardener wants to fence a rectangular flowerbed with $15$ meters of fencing. To two decimal places, what is the largest area that he can make with the fencing?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](images/be/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $15.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{15}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(\\frac{15}{2}-x) \\\\ A & =\\frac{15}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{15}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{15}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{15}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{15}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{15}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{15}{2}-\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{15}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy=\\frac{15}{4}\\cdot \\frac{15}{4}≈14.06\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984bdcd8-65fe-53ce-9365-592fb416acc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399470} {:answer/id #uuid "6f09a46f-378b-5666-b31d-7d594024f410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399471} {:answer/id #uuid "6299c235-6bfa-5841-89fe-0705634d245b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399472} {:answer/id #uuid "2d302c69-b32c-57f8-ae7a-4cd8d92c0343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399473} {:answer/id #uuid "d62b096f-ff9c-5138-8170-e9ee40a546da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "984bdcd8-65fe-53ce-9365-592fb416acc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399470} :answer-field/id #uuid "905988c5-8a51-5ced-aa99-8546923bfeea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f004a40-028a-581c-ab25-3ac8bd7f4fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105281" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y &=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=-1<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}))=1\\cdot 4-(-1)^{2}=3>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})={h}_{11}=1>0.$ Therefore, we have a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3afd00a5-3aad-54a4-9a70-7c2101ecdf05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(e,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399477} {:answer/id #uuid "31d284b3-9581-5047-bd3c-06f5026f21ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399478} {:answer/id #uuid "695ef7e4-8c5b-50c4-8cf4-14edfc45ad32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399479} {:answer/id #uuid "0f6f1aac-15a6-50d6-aa33-e5d5e4a93551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399480} {:answer/id #uuid "c771dbaf-56aa-550b-b408-073f99da90f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(e,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31d284b3-9581-5047-bd3c-06f5026f21ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399478} :answer-field/id #uuid "7119470b-d6b1-5d18-94e0-b83f9d1bc35d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e1b5f6e8-a9c9-5be3-86f7-77f082bfcf04" :question/math-academy-id "q-105338" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2(0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=0\\cdot (-2)-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5103ad76-a94a-5286-8ba5-a77f53f60465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399666} {:answer/id #uuid "8436223b-700a-5cfc-999b-c15dd1c4967a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399667} {:answer/id #uuid "48bbfde1-4a90-5800-90a2-a13b81b7ffae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399668} {:answer/id #uuid "dbee7bb0-5392-5018-a5d9-d29c6d9994e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399669} {:answer/id #uuid "9445423e-a521-575c-b99b-639347c58fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8436223b-700a-5cfc-999b-c15dd1c4967a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399667} :answer-field/id #uuid "689f3bd0-df18-5a92-92e6-a4a5162ad010" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6414dac4-9443-5e38-b246-ae2ca315e478" :question/math-academy-id "q-109302" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=2{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+3t-2,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+3t-2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-18t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-18t+3) \\\\  & =12t-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{18}{12} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{3}{2}) & =6{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-18(\\frac{3}{2})+3 \\\\  & =6(\\frac{9}{4})-27+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-27+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1f8e3ed-64ac-534b-9097-97dd33f2545d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399673} {:answer/id #uuid "106f9321-de03-58f4-8540-f31885a755c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399674} {:answer/id #uuid "084bda93-d0d6-514b-b8c7-73ce821b2053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399675} {:answer/id #uuid "367c159c-f8cf-54ab-9cdc-cfddc4ed2a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399676} {:answer/id #uuid "212ea060-615f-5258-8e09-ac350ea1ca59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{15}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "084bda93-d0d6-514b-b8c7-73ce821b2053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399675} :answer-field/id #uuid "1669c4fe-448a-5ecd-96c2-60941d083824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7704a427-55d1-5f8e-bf57-d941d8544127" :question/math-academy-id "q-109307" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =14t-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(14t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =14-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14-6t & =0 \\\\ -6t & =-14 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{7}{3}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{7}{3}) & =14(\\frac{7}{3})-3{(\\frac{7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{3}-\\frac{49}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b361b7f6-2975-5ed2-b3d6-cb981bcbc5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399762} {:answer/id #uuid "af436aca-c25b-50d1-ae25-749ecc05a446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399763} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4e5843-97cc-5c3b-b643-9e3130891ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399764} {:answer/id #uuid "04e15661-c522-5a93-a20e-dafb0bf87524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399765} {:answer/id #uuid "ab45afe2-3455-59a3-8889-5adbe62eaacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab45afe2-3455-59a3-8889-5adbe62eaacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399766} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5f1bfdb-7442-5063-835b-afe41546d21e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaa71aa3-e92b-5db0-96a6-7043b42bf47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112524" :question/problem "The equation $4{x}^{2}+6x-5=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =5 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{29}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $q=\\frac{29}{4}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{29}{4}=\\frac{32}{4}=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87a1b81f-6a01-51e3-8077-b35d6d3c965e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399984} {:answer/id #uuid "121d4c99-f9fd-560e-b8d1-8cf12f76ef01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399985} {:answer/id #uuid "37c5a8a6-835d-5948-a4e4-bc4371e503af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399986} {:answer/id #uuid "ed218258-9b28-520e-b77f-948add6059b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399987} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5f6dc5-0cb2-55ae-b3d1-11c8ecd253e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "121d4c99-f9fd-560e-b8d1-8cf12f76ef01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399985} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83f0ff3-bc35-5721-8d0b-7a1f7fc531ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0024a60-f6e5-5b56-9272-60dda6ebe058" :question/math-academy-id "q-118389" :question/problem "![](images/37/37434a61262f829160fc2b19fcadb67745a024d589cd1ce318ca4ab90d6ab2d9.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-3-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{(\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+2+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{x}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfcf8936-fe04-5204-8f52-048169f8143c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=13\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400019} {:answer/id #uuid "4354fbe7-0e94-59ad-8bc8-b68fc3254049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=16\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400020} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b0ec1f-4536-5d09-bec6-542d1e1c4e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=28\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400021} {:answer/id #uuid "f65d0d49-5ebc-510e-9648-768a0fdf524d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400022} {:answer/id #uuid "68d9aeeb-dc96-5b04-819a-0cef0a204db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=15\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f65d0d49-5ebc-510e-9648-768a0fdf524d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400022} :answer-field/id #uuid "8281fad4-2620-5bb8-90b0-3efd82e15f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a883694e-b003-511e-8990-ce136a285dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-120778" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12-3{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time  in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=15\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 12-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(2-t)(2+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6t \\\\ {v}^{″}(2) & =-6(2) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(2)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (12-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12t-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=15.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(1)-(1)^{3}+C & =15 \\\\ 11+C & =15\\,⟹\\,C=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=4+12t-{t}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(2)=4+12(2)-(2)^{3}=20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac6379c3-3e1c-5a08-ae54-d99f43489aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400067} {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9efa8-1c48-5867-a1e9-734559b5450d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{2e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400069} {:answer/id #uuid "f85a9aab-60c9-5ec0-8bc2-0588e38d3687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400070} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e29ac-c571-528d-b8c7-bb6ee10f19df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e41e50-ea1d-54b4-8438-62f4c2b9fa61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "138e1f42-3fb6-5013-87c6-eb59c3a912d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122931" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e, \\\\ y & =2(1){e}^{1}-2{e}^{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(e,0).$  For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =2(1)=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,2\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(e,0)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-e) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71b63c40-4166-58be-98ca-c4f5edc13748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400074} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a8ab40-ccbb-5118-b2d9-f78348093f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400075} {:answer/id #uuid "b749b256-812c-5226-8d12-e7a772eb2ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400076} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a93f8e-9a44-5007-89a0-efaebde7602c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400077} {:answer/id #uuid "bded8aee-c9b6-50a7-9080-88cf30b26b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{3{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8a8ab40-ccbb-5118-b2d9-f78348093f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400075} :answer-field/id #uuid "d769d84c-e8c5-56e0-8e28-ef7348f0bb6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce632c36-0a89-59ca-a425-8c378bad7972" :question/math-academy-id "q-123020" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae210abc4028440aa9c822510d68f0ab0b8ba4b9f5806dd299149e953ccd1b21.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={t}^{3}-4,\\,y=2t$\nfrom $t=-2$ to $t=0$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4)=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2})^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95a19ab2-d61e-5cd4-9296-0ccc2a2ce9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+32}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400081} {:answer/id #uuid "6f516eb5-80db-56b9-88c4-8fec44175002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400082} {:answer/id #uuid "d04936f6-e3a3-5955-87a3-ae992e7c288a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400083} {:answer/id #uuid "25a5d7fc-f3f7-50d4-9122-01398e9fb385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400084} {:answer/id #uuid "4e16c553-ec8a-5568-b27c-e4f4689e3032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e16c553-ec8a-5568-b27c-e4f4689e3032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400085} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bd27ca-b51f-55c8-a2b1-03c787e77a4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a37e6009-e864-56bc-93ae-b166a2b05514" :question/math-academy-id "q-123027" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=8-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(8-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+64-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221b2a78-3ed9-5fcc-a904-b72ed2d8cda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400088} {:answer/id #uuid "2037ceca-da9b-5e6c-99af-f9e9635c71f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+6{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400089} {:answer/id #uuid "f6282b39-25dd-527c-8c08-c5ccbd4d5888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+12{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400090} {:answer/id #uuid "3954ccd4-113c-522e-8865-af8dedf5924f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400091} {:answer/id #uuid "5f536300-dcae-55c0-9423-568faa002b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f536300-dcae-55c0-9423-568faa002b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400092} :answer-field/id #uuid "065d677c-9998-56e4-8b57-ce841d04bf36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30eadd5a-469c-54ec-b683-aec3a996fde4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123030" :question/problem "![](images/88/8868658c9bc7195f435a0109fce26b8a4fa39324c0a9a39e3fb644c5f02cdc9b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57570228-8466-52cb-a11f-f04e18706442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470931} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8642b5-04f8-5c37-a14b-aed7ee7a7659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57570228-8466-52cb-a11f-f04e18706442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470931} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffa6fff-c4b5-5b71-b3d9-ad640d9a3224" :answer-field/key "${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471971} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d09e71-31ad-54bf-8cea-1a8f785b490a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470933} {:answer/id #uuid "022b26c2-7d25-5b06-a964-476aa350c72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "022b26c2-7d25-5b06-a964-476aa350c72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470934} :answer-field/id #uuid "528c0afa-a7a6-540b-be79-d029b67039c3" :answer-field/key "${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471972} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6fcdbe-5b61-5426-9e91-ddd477fc741b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470935} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe5aeb4-873c-584e-ac06-a0748a68d7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c6fcdbe-5b61-5426-9e91-ddd477fc741b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470935} :answer-field/id #uuid "e500961e-1c74-5a51-95f4-6ce13652944a" :answer-field/key "${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}$ for $n\\ge 1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4187c6ec-17d9-5b83-9a20-6aff01cc94de" :question/math-academy-id "q-123315" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$”,  the dominant term in the denominator is $2{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$”, the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}-n)}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-n}{0-1} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}$ for $n\\ge 1$”,  the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$” diverges."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04289a9d-17cf-573a-9701-d41c64ef2496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(5+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400109} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd6606e-b516-5f6f-9f4b-c6f100ea81db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400110} {:answer/id #uuid "41205926-9dd0-53e7-b1aa-ade02572e0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400111} {:answer/id #uuid "be2d7cad-36f0-55ab-b08f-a68a53ffb570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400112} {:answer/id #uuid "4933aa14-2d33-5b3a-973b-f6175f55c3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be2d7cad-36f0-55ab-b08f-a68a53ffb570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400112} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bff7a0b-d96e-5598-95de-cbbd3ac2a012" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffdfd696-312e-58bb-aa35-858898cb0c00" :question/math-academy-id "q-123519" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(4-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4259c2a-e037-547f-ba6f-c09ebc5c66e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470937} {:answer/id #uuid "93b837fb-dba3-5553-903e-a4ce54621dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4259c2a-e037-547f-ba6f-c09ebc5c66e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470937} :answer-field/id #uuid "b837ace8-23d9-5324-878d-853d536782fd" :answer-field/key "$-1+1-1+1-⋯$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471974} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9227e56-d78e-5113-b51b-4b43a7893634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470939} {:answer/id #uuid "76dafe01-8b75-51aa-95ee-e47c3e5b805d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76dafe01-8b75-51aa-95ee-e47c3e5b805d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470940} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dcaf81f-0ef1-5a8a-bef8-bc4b434fc677" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471975} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c50ac6cc-b478-5878-9ce1-12b1bc9ba137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470941} {:answer/id #uuid "e8414a63-aa61-5abf-b05d-7a72e20164ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c50ac6cc-b478-5878-9ce1-12b1bc9ba137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470941} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e23d4f5-0d62-58e8-b892-40daa1d8e271" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55c1789a-3f1b-5ebc-a34c-eaab270f436c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124912" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $-1+1-1+1-⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\begin{gathered}0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.11}{0.1} \\\\  & =1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent  because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$ converges."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1e516a-fd02-539c-a55b-1d53b977ac63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400158} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd19dff-a905-5735-9496-24d7b4eee41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400159} {:answer/id #uuid "7175eeec-eda1-5a92-99be-f76806feffc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400160} {:answer/id #uuid "14302d62-f56c-5807-8418-d9e29a49a8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400161} {:answer/id #uuid "df3a9c05-b642-53bc-aee7-47f4b93b0bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df3a9c05-b642-53bc-aee7-47f4b93b0bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400162} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec6cfae-f16c-5f01-8f87-2f00e0b258ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bb1887b-828c-58c3-9b10-3c0e7dc6d40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124965" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd41c969aee2e7c04c9034106978d8522d6a715733098bfde87abbdb861da7a8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4},$ what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/49/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4})}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbeb0313-5651-561b-a4b7-42ac4f88f1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =8\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400165} {:answer/id #uuid "1a165698-e8f8-506c-9756-3955aee8c79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400166} {:answer/id #uuid "32a56d6b-1f3c-53ed-9db5-88987f60fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400167} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3e631f-f822-5565-8077-5a496dd0604b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400168} {:answer/id #uuid "0944c75f-3871-5029-b2ac-06fe51638caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-8\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32a56d6b-1f3c-53ed-9db5-88987f60fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400167} :answer-field/id #uuid "77272688-3fb6-5eaf-8b2d-59ba189820e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c006e542-29a2-5102-8308-98f4920ebd17" :question/math-academy-id "q-125004" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/5c/5c98414bbe0e1e373864e9ec1d6b8667bb0032c8824f20bed1e6f9b9079c4d02.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8})}=-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b515481-27aa-50cd-a614-2b3b3c8e9916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400235} {:answer/id #uuid "70cb914f-6e67-52a8-8099-833f6e52d19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400236} {:answer/id #uuid "246bb1dc-7057-5890-bb32-5ae992573b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400237} {:answer/id #uuid "a615fbac-40a1-5a96-905b-a1b1e6c1fe27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400238} {:answer/id #uuid "92822cb5-bcbd-5e16-981e-b28bb3550efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246bb1dc-7057-5890-bb32-5ae992573b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400237} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e1c6021-571a-58eb-ae1a-ae2e4f0fcf7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e567d72e-c85f-58b7-9ff5-c8e3c41c5382" :question/math-academy-id "q-126774" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}=\\frac{3x}{{1}^{3/2}}-\\frac{{3}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{3}^{3}{x}^{3}}{{3}^{3/2}}-\\frac{{3}^{4}{x}^{4}}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x{n}^{3/2}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =3|-x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{3},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{3},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{(\\frac{3}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.   So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}{(\\frac{3}{3})}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.   So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}].$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549a5daf-5666-553f-82f5-27205446194a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{4}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400256} {:answer/id #uuid "c59d759c-090a-569e-a841-2f3f881a6cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400257} {:answer/id #uuid "81b3de1b-3b6d-5810-a6e2-9923ca999f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400258} {:answer/id #uuid "12b9fcee-9cc1-5cd7-9dcf-0488b3381528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-(x-2)+\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400259} {:answer/id #uuid "49ec3922-a5e3-5d3a-8e40-816bba0da596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(x-2)-\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81b3de1b-3b6d-5810-a6e2-9923ca999f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400258} :answer-field/id #uuid "9533d8c7-2fb9-5174-8f0a-9eef9e450415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b7eb3fc-d271-5f71-a892-30b0a27230ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-126844" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ about the point $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ f(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(\\frac{1}{2})(x-2)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-2)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813ea027-299b-510a-86a9-2e76ce0edeec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.739" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400270} {:answer/id #uuid "81e7917c-2e81-580f-949d-985ad24febfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400271} {:answer/id #uuid "aee0f806-2311-54bd-9a9b-bb6e04c3b17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.744" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400272} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4972b2-7545-5695-be8e-1e853fe55f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.746" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400273} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2db0c1-d929-521e-913f-2d2d27ccf71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.732" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81e7917c-2e81-580f-949d-985ad24febfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400271} :answer-field/id #uuid "4855ece8-350c-52ea-af76-5afe755310a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fcccafb-96d2-5248-a5bc-581af0a17196" :question/math-academy-id "q-126877" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1+(2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(4)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & ≈1+2(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})+2{(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{200} \\\\  & =0.735\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b07b3524-f4dc-51f3-b58d-511019f380d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400319} {:answer/id #uuid "a90fc3d7-ae16-5eee-afb7-67cae2c4704b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400320} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad10364-e2bf-5ff4-864d-439d03c0d490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400321} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f29039-8134-5396-8f11-6539728a73f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400322} {:answer/id #uuid "472e4b77-2d2c-5148-8426-4a481212167a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4.2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b07b3524-f4dc-51f3-b58d-511019f380d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400319} :answer-field/id #uuid "301c7d8d-c9ed-5aaa-9881-a1b6ba08393d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "150d488e-5edb-5db7-b1b9-30f4afcb814f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127770" :question/problem "Consider a game of chance with $4$ prizes ($$1$, $$2$, $$3$, and $$4$) where each outcome has a specific probability of happening, shown in the table below. \n\n| $\\text{Number}$ | $\\text{Probability}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$1$ | $40%$ |\n| $$2$ | $30%$ |\n| $$3$ | $20%$ |\n| $$4$ | $10%$ |\n\nWhat is the expected profit per game?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game. Using the given probabilities, let's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{2}{5})+2(\\frac{3}{10})+3(\\frac{1}{5})+4(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{4}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{10} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5c6186e-dc96-51d6-82cf-5c90683fa84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439503} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd21dd5-0725-5fc9-a948-255a7375e687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cd21dd5-0725-5fc9-a948-255a7375e687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439504} :answer-field/id #uuid "aae52bae-eb88-5ee0-a6c0-ddf96c9c5413" :answer-field/key "$(-3,-3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f138de-76d3-51c6-b70f-37f4ae000dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439505} {:answer/id #uuid "abd8a918-fae9-5155-b1e4-9387ec218bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26f138de-76d3-51c6-b70f-37f4ae000dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439505} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea60b4f-c7d5-5ba9-b54b-a47a64f292ec" :answer-field/key "$(-3,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707f2204-105d-5f98-a7c5-02aaedc7a057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439507} {:answer/id #uuid "d8586660-fd7e-5eda-9284-01ae5a7a40b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "707f2204-105d-5f98-a7c5-02aaedc7a057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439507} :answer-field/id #uuid "192ed7e0-867a-5042-9244-595a5145fa06" :answer-field/key "$(3,-3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440111} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7553f1-861f-51c0-8547-71189b4ae228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439509} {:answer/id #uuid "3f812865-94f7-5778-aa60-73b89c449256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa7553f1-861f-51c0-8547-71189b4ae228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439509} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd2c2d4-80d6-5681-8a2f-d724cd733e7a" :answer-field/key "$(3,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a633d445-62c8-5864-b7bf-d694cd1cd56c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128519" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j=0\\,i+0\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 81+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-27 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point: $(-3,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400676} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c15084-f362-505f-8950-6df3b2548ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.74$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400677} {:answer/id #uuid "e01f491c-202f-5132-a3f5-3f0f2930aa15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.65$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400678} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea6bf2-0abb-5dd3-8132-52b2488943a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.57$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400679} {:answer/id #uuid "7b383bc1-6fb9-59e8-b057-fb6dccdf74a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.79$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400676} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb566678-fdf6-552d-aad6-4bf9b9328ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42e998b4-da64-533d-80e4-6303495d71cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132852" :question/problem "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years.\nAdditionally, $3%$ of batteries last less than $1.75$ years. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<1.75)=0.03.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.75) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)=-1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.8808 \\\\ 1.75-\\mu  & =-0.9404 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈2.69.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6028dff8-a045-5c36-b03a-7cc1e9d875d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.27$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400683} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfc67ac-b865-541b-abd3-49ba35181b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.73$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400684} {:answer/id #uuid "7922a355-3dcf-5ee9-a956-87ba8d17b65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4.85$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400685} {:answer/id #uuid "be6f8eda-cb13-5113-8a9a-1637beaf9d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4.41$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400686} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddb9a99-6c2a-5cfd-b39f-b1b603fa424c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.38$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6028dff8-a045-5c36-b03a-7cc1e9d875d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.27$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400683} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f6174e-7ddd-5db0-b8a0-9648c670a8d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50c01113-7eba-59c9-b302-374966a56f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-132856" :question/problem "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.7.$ In addition, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.75\\mu $ and $$1.25\\mu .$ Find the average cinema admission fee, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent an admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & =1.8808 \\\\ 0.25\\mu  & =1.31656 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.31656}{0.25} \\\\  & ≈5.27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d912e9d9-60dd-53f8-84b5-6a01262be9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400925} {:answer/id #uuid "5e69372e-e868-57ad-81a9-235542604c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400926} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3cf5dc-1a5e-5ca5-bb04-89cb5648d292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400927} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f4c2df-fdff-5c81-86c1-ed79a5590ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400928} {:answer/id #uuid "ee524a7c-b00b-54ff-b0db-1379d3f0eb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d912e9d9-60dd-53f8-84b5-6a01262be9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400925} :answer-field/id #uuid "a83cb541-9ba7-558c-804e-5b78b4a327b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c5b5131-1801-5dfb-aff8-f50ca940df15" :question/math-academy-id "q-141310" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{4}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c77b2f36-6be8-50c0-ae22-868fa6cf86a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401016} {:answer/id #uuid "09d3bae4-8fa4-5410-960c-88cdf9ccbafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401017} {:answer/id #uuid "e29d1f8d-e673-5fab-ab5a-b94d3c279c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401018} {:answer/id #uuid "d634400e-7fcc-5b2f-8882-b33255d21d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401019} {:answer/id #uuid "33483e89-3247-5b5b-b6c3-9a62773834b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c77b2f36-6be8-50c0-ae22-868fa6cf86a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401016} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d94100e-63ab-58cf-b2c0-0f485eafe140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a434871-4fea-52cd-80a4-1fd81c159fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15133" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a760f55b6437a39010e4d1a00bdb5fb5390d848dd74347e7736e2ac49cd562b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}+(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+144{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d63901ad-848e-5f8f-b7f4-a6f0cabcd6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401023} {:answer/id #uuid "802cfcd0-a88b-5f37-b901-56f2fa1fec34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401024} {:answer/id #uuid "3d75caa2-eae0-5f0c-a250-6aa6ab5b7726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401025} {:answer/id #uuid "892fe229-a636-5af2-bb7c-28f7e30053e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401026} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7af40e-74ed-51e2-be9d-1cf5c95c728a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d7af40e-74ed-51e2-be9d-1cf5c95c728a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401027} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aecd8d8-445d-5023-ad24-6e7f3a0a5ec4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c659d13d-a024-51d5-b83b-b004bef94416" :question/math-academy-id "q-15136" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1+{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "925080f9-1488-5ed3-9988-57bac8e25aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+2t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401036} {:answer/id #uuid "8b14ae74-d72b-5c6f-bfb4-9fdf19b4b592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401037} {:answer/id #uuid "c911c453-c1b3-506d-a7de-c8341e32d5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401038} {:answer/id #uuid "69aeb839-370f-503b-8dde-d870cf822808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401039} {:answer/id #uuid "e1915447-2ced-5598-8af5-d918f3a8a810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+24t+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c911c453-c1b3-506d-a7de-c8341e32d5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401038} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac60323-fbe0-5435-bfb2-619a71c7a175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dddf8a10-d360-5144-aae4-5bd6e2bd6915" :question/math-academy-id "q-15174" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}-2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2}+2t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2}-2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}-12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{18{t}^{4}+8{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b85309c-59d7-5810-84c4-7ea68f827ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401043} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd04e4b-26ba-5e0d-9091-43c5f06e6bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401044} {:answer/id #uuid "eb350998-6cb3-5e21-b128-0e21ffcf76a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401045} {:answer/id #uuid "9f297950-b005-5b22-b845-7190b859cf3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401046} {:answer/id #uuid "a99448ba-c640-5e57-a298-7dcd50a71e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb350998-6cb3-5e21-b128-0e21ffcf76a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401045} :answer-field/id #uuid "9286f2bc-6874-57c4-a822-f4b4566f80e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a2f2890-829e-5660-aa0e-71dea52c1f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-15178" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca290e8fd51edddf4381ec267b8e0a5692d0e52bd2a36b7d43ab89cbeb5cf00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c46a2e-4e00-5991-99c9-9dc68c9a9598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401050} {:answer/id #uuid "2499ce46-f979-5a96-92be-fd0c7ac31bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401051} {:answer/id #uuid "61eb1149-6ab5-5e3a-8377-404854e1b082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401052} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed903c6-dd0e-5b89-95d6-7cb6b868a842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401053} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b764bb-1a8d-5c44-bf30-3132c5443550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2499ce46-f979-5a96-92be-fd0c7ac31bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401051} :answer-field/id #uuid "d340f787-388c-5296-b3a4-86e39060db0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "243b404d-a4f2-57e8-bc56-d916439edf0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15182" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d683c3960705fa32d11826008a9844c51c8e59e38f5158fce7f4e44c31b97ca9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bead1cb0-40bc-5682-8aea-b1306552b221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401064} {:answer/id #uuid "86056585-0e06-5b86-b0c0-36b6f0375c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401065} {:answer/id #uuid "7c15ff06-b974-57e0-9d0d-91476444df9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401066} {:answer/id #uuid "552b9d8c-6c80-5fa5-9717-bf514c6bbbca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401067} {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401068} :answer-field/id #uuid "4182a726-9587-5280-b0ae-9a47c4e9e265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16fc9f31-33f6-5972-9529-3afa5f1e1dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-15465" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1+0} \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed482a3a-a562-53c9-9952-b7f4ae64a9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401228} {:answer/id #uuid "292ba469-4e9c-5812-b40c-813e27620124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3y}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401229} {:answer/id #uuid "1139e147-84fe-5bb5-a30a-874f5c401de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401230} {:answer/id #uuid "25a694b0-85e4-50a9-9c0d-fb4f6db18268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401231} {:answer/id #uuid "60287cb2-73a4-5d70-9afa-49321e3ddf3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3x}({x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a694b0-85e4-50a9-9c0d-fb4f6db18268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401231} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2d063b-2ed3-5177-a74e-32306bee1975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e5c369-0d01-5527-87a6-9d6d08f8cf0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161314" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-x+y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y(-x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fef4addc-f714-5c73-ba79-b8bbf017a6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401235} {:answer/id #uuid "32ada5ea-3508-5fad-8e67-d2275d868693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{4}{y}^{4}+{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401236} {:answer/id #uuid "0adb517f-11c6-5796-89c8-e402d79f5011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401237} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c7988a-9786-511c-9fd8-d31cb890bb80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4x+4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401238} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5ced82-50c7-5506-b6d6-5d386e556ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0adb517f-11c6-5796-89c8-e402d79f5011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401237} :answer-field/id #uuid "63dd6eb1-0216-5ca0-946f-dc8a7cfe578a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5d98cd9-310e-5d64-ad53-16fad07f2de6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161320" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e70215a-59ba-5fc4-8869-08fd079debf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401304} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6baa46-2362-5360-8c5f-7031d62e1411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401305} {:answer/id #uuid "7adad8b8-d3ef-52ef-b487-e4018ac1507b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401306} {:answer/id #uuid "06bed278-63d1-5662-b5c3-13bc65e1ffc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401307} {:answer/id #uuid "03678aa6-e1ae-5bec-8d2a-faf8e1de8ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(uv)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e70215a-59ba-5fc4-8869-08fd079debf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401304} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b627816-1293-53a9-a8e8-93bf2fb0fcef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4004275e-59e4-54d2-bbaa-29203ac29308" :question/math-academy-id "q-161879" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z-3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69eaa3fe-4c03-532c-9d1a-cd84b8703ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401338} {:answer/id #uuid "460112aa-e36b-52a6-a02b-783fee9d05b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401339} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fbcee-821a-5cd9-8a6e-f9412bd4ab5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401340} {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} {:answer/id #uuid "1540b931-3add-5e5c-99b4-77aa9c86e5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} :answer-field/id #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162365" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a316d2cb-bab1-518a-9a6c-3956bfcbcd26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401595} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec09427-50e7-5ea6-a6c5-d462ec4a977e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401596} {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401597} {:answer/id #uuid "ed368aed-25dd-5df6-b266-89719aec6636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401598} {:answer/id #uuid "d50e40c4-e8c2-5ba6-ad0e-dc0b92fad230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401597} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e70783-1025-5acd-82ba-24685ceb80f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b98dba6-8029-58a2-a81c-ae05e5ca1860" :question/math-academy-id "q-170347" :question/problem "![](images/71/71d9df0708a77639f3f7b2640ea2a03cebbfd72e6cfb3369c07d83a89eba6508.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Therefore, $c=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a58988e-9e60-53e1-9ccc-6195ac2bdc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401868} {:answer/id #uuid "c4824b8b-2ed7-5c3c-ae44-9e7e39c2adaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401869} {:answer/id #uuid "8e755153-eb7c-5c80-ac13-6bd2f55ebc06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401870} {:answer/id #uuid "8dadc22b-b156-5944-b045-f3c498f81787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401871} {:answer/id #uuid "89771be0-5923-5fe1-8923-30d66256f236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89771be0-5923-5fe1-8923-30d66256f236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401872} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d97d84-3165-5293-95f8-8ad9ae1b81ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a60afb71-a1f2-5589-86e8-84aaf38f221a" :question/math-academy-id "q-21353" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4aa2bca8faab077c47fb968882579233babe7d323ba3120088e8ea5599fbd570.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={e}^{t},\\,y=t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c3666f3-ba98-51a9-b678-f25e043d3a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401917} {:answer/id #uuid "e8039e0c-f293-5ce4-b395-a4064a0685bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401918} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f24666-89d0-5332-bb2a-21f966094be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}-3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401919} {:answer/id #uuid "a479a80c-f0e1-5b4f-9f67-06b99cd086bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401920} {:answer/id #uuid "266f8f59-63e6-5980-a4b9-749b4fb8201c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "266f8f59-63e6-5980-a4b9-749b4fb8201c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401921} :answer-field/id #uuid "828a7f8a-1289-571e-8aa4-3a396be14d44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6948e752-4360-51fe-b06f-c6885f4f282f" :question/math-academy-id "q-27482" :question/problem "![](images/c0/c091fc5d742ddca3090725974f00ff7da77b1530d601bb82e288518b27970b0a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.5.$ Therefore, $c=2.5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402135} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe81525-8780-55b2-9e79-394a40fd9a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402136} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9dd8e3-5764-58b2-9ff2-4b88c32898f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$40$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402137} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5c6a61-12a4-5352-8fcc-e3319bbc18bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402138} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb667f2-6fca-5729-a7fd-9dfe18862602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402135} :answer-field/id #uuid "63531a29-5d3e-5f5f-97ce-ef01b7d03202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4fd0915-73cc-575a-b3cc-4a156a96dd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-335542" :question/problem "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a62be6e9-5300-5837-bb21-616f9afabd11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$570$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402142} {:answer/id #uuid "52f73195-6ad4-5063-9185-1d0ce506d0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$560$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402143} {:answer/id #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402144} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb7ad78-9e83-5378-a008-fb209e8c0c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$550$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402145} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3362f7-e0ab-59c9-813b-230e3fd73524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$590$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402144} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e99660a-207e-56cc-af0c-7810e8c04e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee0a87d3-d3e9-539a-9074-68a374d85d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335590" :question/problem "A small bakery produces loaves of bread. The owner knows that the bakery can produce a maximum of $600$ loaves a day; however, current production is below this maximum. Since she started monitoring, the bakers produced $480$ loaves and $500$ loaves on days $4$ and $8,$ respectively. How many loaves do they produce on day $40?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the bread production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the bread production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible bread production and its current bread production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the bakery, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ loaves, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(600-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{600-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{600-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $4,$ the bread production was $480$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(4)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & =480 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $8,$ the bread production was $500$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(8)=500,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(8) & =500 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-8k} & =500 \\\\ K{e}^{-8k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-8k}} & =\\frac{120}{100} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} & =120 \\\\ K & =120{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} \\\\ K & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=600-144{e}^{-(t/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of loaves baked on day $t=40$ is\n\n$$\nE(40)=600-144{e}^{-(40/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}≈576.743…≈577\\,\\text{loaves},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f7ba4e-e115-562d-8f0b-e2fb3c50999d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402149} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} {:answer/id #uuid "3c36fd19-5696-5a1e-9dc9-b04f43bf5c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402151} {:answer/id #uuid "31324eeb-37b1-5843-9dfa-024be7517c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402152} {:answer/id #uuid "11e8a292-e74d-5f79-93dd-d159881ed802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} :answer-field/id #uuid "450a3a23-c54b-5ed0-90a2-deef088dea63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44cded13-6bc7-52ea-8fcc-d6fe583c3d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-335619" :question/problem "A national park is monitoring the number of people who have downloaded a new park app. The system can support a maximum of $800$ users, but the total is still below this limit. Since the park began tracking downloads, the number of users reached $620$ on day $3$ and $720$ on day $9.$ On what day will the number of users reach $760?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of users, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of app users $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of users the system can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ users, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the number of users was $620.$ So, applying the condition $P(3)=620,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =620 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-3k} & =620 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the number of users was $720.$ So, applying the condition $P(9)=720,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =720 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-9k} & =720 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{180}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} & =180 \\\\ K & =180{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} \\\\ K & =180\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ K & =180\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-270{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$  be the day when the app reaches $760$ users. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =760 \\\\ 800-270{e}^{-kd} & =760 \\\\ 40 & =270{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{40}{270} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{4}{27} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the number of users will be $760$ on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})}=14.1285…≈14,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386007b3-91ce-5982-8534-d31233322fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.3$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402223} {:answer/id #uuid "0a01f341-43f3-528c-b811-0f8051a23c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402224} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac92552-dc2b-5d29-986a-281a4e64330d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.4$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402225} {:answer/id #uuid "c1738319-0cbe-5aa8-beb9-61d8c99fe62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402226} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2f0725-683a-50fc-855c-66c7d9ba756f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.2$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "386007b3-91ce-5982-8534-d31233322fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.3$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402223} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ae4df5-40ba-5454-aa69-0f62becda821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b562af2b-8343-5000-806c-d01887adea11" :question/math-academy-id "q-339222" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $N(t)$ to an e-learning platform varies according to the differential equation ${N}^{′}(t)=rN(t),$ where $t$ is the time since the platform was launched, measured in weeks, and $r>0.$ It is known that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks. How long does it take for the number of subscribers to triple after the platform is launched? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the number of subscribers when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of subscribers equals $3{N}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N({t}_{1}) & =3{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=3{N}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks the platform is launched. Therefore,\n\n$$\nN(4)=2{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{4r}=2{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{4r} & =2{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{4r} & =2\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{4r} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈6.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10efe48b-a9f4-5aa8-8700-8513aef294db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.4$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402230} {:answer/id #uuid "9483f82e-0758-5787-8ce2-48f26c64a23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402231} {:answer/id #uuid "85698021-f6eb-5674-af9e-26f88bf35ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402232} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0b804f-53f8-5360-9835-166b54051187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.1$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402233} {:answer/id #uuid "85d74bd3-72ef-5c17-a201-f97592fcc512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85698021-f6eb-5674-af9e-26f88bf35ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402232} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa46205e-ab90-5810-a697-993aab9f4c54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8375c335-8cf2-5c82-b2c9-770890e7d65a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339226" :question/problem "The value $V(t)$ of a tech device varies according to the differential equation ${V}^{′}(t)=-rV(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r>0.$ It is known that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. How long does it take for the value to reduce to one-fifth of its original value? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nV(t)={V}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${V}_{0}$ is the value when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the value equals $\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. Therefore,\n\n$$\nV(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r}=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -r & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈2.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56be28be-e705-54a4-a778-9f6ec8ce939a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402265} {:answer/id #uu